  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above-normal temperatures with dry conditions \ncontinue tomorrow.\n\nSfc high pressure is sliding offshore this afternoon while an UL \nridge builds over the east coast. Breezy SW flow and height rises \nhave led to a warmer than average day across the area. Latest obs \nshow temperatures in the mid 80s under mostly sunny skies. Tomorrow, \nthe high stays just offshore while the ridge aloft is slowly \nsuppressed and pushed east through Sat morning ahead of the next \nsystem. Highs are a few degrees warmer tomorrow with much of the \narea likely reaching 90F. Record highs (included in the Climate \nsection) are generally in the mid to upper 90s for Oct 2nd, so not \nexpecting any broken records, though it could be close in ORF and \nECG. Averages for early October, for reference, are in the low to \nmid 70s. A cold front sinking through the area Friday night will \nbring about a quick pattern change and return to near-average\ntemps.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An unsettled weather pattern with cooler and \nwetter conditions is likely this weekend, followed by a gradual \ndrying trend and cool temperatures next week.\n\nThe first of two cold fronts is expected to slide through the local \narea Friday night into early Saturday. Not a ton of support aloft \nbehind this initial front, but it is enough to suppress the ridge \ninto more of a zonal flow over the local region. Rain \nassociated with the system could move into northern counties as \nearly as late Fri night, but chances for widespread showers \nmostly hold off until Saturday mid-day/afternoon. Chances will \nbe highest in the piedmont and northern counties at ~70% and \ntapering to ~30% in the far southeast. The timing of the front \nhas trended a bit faster, which will likely mean cooler temps \nthan previously forecast, especially across the south. Forecast \nhighs for Sat are in the mid 60s across the north and low to mid\n70s across the southern counties. \n\nThe bulk of the QPF and more widespread overrunning rainfall arrive \nSaturday night through Monday morning ahead of a second cold front \nwhich is anticipated on Monday. There is high model agreement that \nSun will be wet with widespread showers/rain as tropical moisture \nstreams into the area between the cold fronts. PoPs of 70-80% spread \nacross the area Sun morning and will taper off NW to SE late Sun \ninto early Mon. QPF through Sun evening has a widespread 1-1.5\" with \nthe highest amounts concentrated along and west of the I-95 corridor \nsouth of Richmond. NBM probs for over an inch show 60-75% for the \nbulk of the area (highest SW) and the 12z Euro Ens and GEFS has 30-\n50%, which is an increase from the 00z runs. Not expecting much in \nthe way of thunder due to very little instability, but cannot rule \nout a rumble or two across the southeast on Sunday. A strong area of \ncool high pressure builds in behind the front, resulting in cooler \nand drier conditions for next week. Temps will be at or even below \nseasonal norms with overnight lows dipping into the 40s inland by \nmid week.",
